The Domain Name System (DNS) is a core Internet service, but its resolution behavior is governed by subtle interactions among delegation, aliases, wildcards, and negative responses. These interactions make DNS configurations difficult to reason about formally, especially when multiple authoritative zones jointly determine the outcome of a query. Existing verification efforts have shown the value of formal DNS analysis, but they are typically centered on procedural models or complex executable semantics rather than a compact declarative foundation. We present SemDNS, a declarative semantics for authoritative DNS resolution. SemDNS models zone records as relational facts and expresses DNS behavior as layered logical inference over those facts. The semantics progressively capture intra-zone structure, authority and delegation boundaries, alias handling, wildcard matching, and final resolution judgments. This design makes DNS reasoning explicit, modular, and amenable to fixed-point evaluation in a logic-programming setting. Overall, SemDNS provides a clear formal basis for understanding and analyzing authoritative DNS behavior across interacting zones, and offers a principled foundation for future DNS verification and analysis systems.